Chromium is available. I can do a headless smoke test: load the player page directly with query params? My app doesn't support query params for autoload — to test the player view I'd need to click. Better: headless chromium with --dump-dom or --headless --screenshot to verify the selection screen renders without JS errors. For the player view, I could use --run-all-compositor-stages... without interaction it's hard. Alternative: temporarily drive via CDP? Simplest: use chromium headless with virtual-time and evaluate console errors by loading page with `--enable-logging --js-flags` ... hmm.

Option: add "autostart" query parameters (also genuinely useful! e.g. `?video=videos/fight.mp4&ecg=beispieldaten&record=1` deep-link). That makes smoke-testing AND user quick-starts easier. Let me add: on load, parse URLSearchParams: if video & ecg present → call startPlayback directly (records auto: fetch measurements, pick record). This is a small, useful feature and enables headless verification of the player view rendering via screenshot.

Implement in app.js:

```js
async function autostart() {
  const p = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
  const v = p.get("video"), f = p.get("ecg");
  if (!v || !f) return;
  state.videoPath = v; state.ecgFolder = f;
  $("pick-video-label").textContent = v; ...remove empty
  await loadRecords();
  const r = p.get("record"); if (r) { state.recordNo = Number(r); $("record-select").value = r; }
  if (p.get("offset")) { state.offset = Number(p.get("offset")); $("offset-input").value = state.offset; }
  refreshStart();
  if (!$("btn-start").disabled) startPlayback();
}
autostart();
```

Then headless test: create a real tiny mp4 (can I encode with ffmpeg? check availability). If no ffmpeg, video element won't load metadata but the page will still render (video poster area black, ecg chart drawn). For the screenshot test that's fine — but the ecg time is video.currentTime=0 anyway. Actually even without playable video the chart draws with t=offset=0. 

Check ffmpeg. If present, generate 90s test video (color+cone pattern, h264). That would let the user immediately try without their own video. Hmm — generating a sample video into the repo? Might be useful: videos/beispiel.mp4 (tiny, e.g. 90 s at 320x180, ~100 KB). The measurement is 84 s; a video of similar length would demo nicely. But committing binary video files to their git repo unprompted? It's genuinely useful for testing the sync app, small size, but I'd rather keep the repo clean and just use it for my local smoke test (videos/ is gitignored anyway). Good: videos/{dtox} gitignored → generate test video there, headless screenshot, don't commit. 

Then README update + docker-compose note. Let me add autostart params + a "Deep link" doc in README.

